# Job Hunter Extension — Agent Rules (Cline)

# These rules are automatically injected into every conversation context.

# You MUST follow them. No exceptions without explicit user approval.

## TypeScript Rules (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

- strict: true, noUncheckedIndexedAccess: true, noImplicitReturns: true
- All interface properties must be readonly
- NEVER use 'any' — use 'unknown' and narrow with type guards
- Explicit return types on all exported functions
- const over let, NEVER var
- No non-null assertions (!) — use proper type guards

## Code Quality

- Max function: 50 lines (utils), 30 lines (services/handlers)
- Max cyclomatic complexity: 10
- Max function parameters: 4
- No magic numbers — extract to named constants
- Prefer early returns over deep nesting

## Code Patterns (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

### TypeScript Strictness

- NEVER use `any` — use `unknown` and narrow with type guards
- ALWAYS add explicit return types on exported functions
- NEVER use non-null assertion (`!`) — use proper type guards or optional chaining
- NEVER use `==` — always `===` and `!==`
- NEVER write an `async` function without at least one `await` expression inside it
- NEVER leave unused variables — prefix with `_` if intentionally unused
- ALWAYS use `const` over `let`, NEVER `var`

### Boolean & Null Handling

- NEVER coerce objects/strings/numbers to booleans. Use explicit checks: `arr.length > 0` not `if (arr)`, `str !== ''` not `if (str)`
- ALWAYS use optional chaining (`?.`) instead of nested `&&` checks
- ALWAYS use nullish coalescing (`??`) instead of `||` for defaults
- NEVER write unnecessary conditionals
- ALWAYS exhaustively check all switch cases

### Function & Code Structure

- Max function body: 30 lines for services/handlers, 50 lines for utils (blank lines & comments don't count)
- Max cyclomatic complexity: 10
- Max nesting depth: 3
- Max parameters: 4
- NEVER use magic numbers (except: 0, 1, -1, 2) — extract to named constants
- NEVER use `else` after `return`
- NEVER use `return await` — just `return`
- NEVER reassign function parameters
- ALWAYS add a blank line before `return` statements
- ALWAYS add a blank line between declarations and block-like statements (if/for/while)

### Console & Debugging

- NEVER use `console.log` — only `console.warn` and `console.error` are allowed
- NEVER leave `debugger` statements, `alert()`, `eval()`, or `implied eval` in code

### Imports & Formatting

- NEVER use default exports — always named exports
- ALWAYS sort imports: builtin → external → internal → parent → sibling → index, alphabetized
- NEVER create import cycles
- NEVER have duplicate imports
- NEVER use useless path segments like `./index` or `../` when not needed
- No trailing spaces, max 1 consecutive blank line, always end files with a newline
- NEVER start a file with a blank line

## Extension-Specific Rules

- This is a Chrome Extension (Manifest V3) — all code runs in a browser extension context
- LLM_API_KEY in `.env` only — never committed, never hardcoded in source
- NEVER log API keys, tokens, or passwords
- No secrets in code — env vars only
- Content scripts scoped to job description containers, never full page DOM
- Use browser extension APIs (`browser.storage`, `browser.runtime`, `browser.tabs`) for persistence and messaging — the build targets Chrome/Firefox/Safari via WXT, which exposes the same surface across browsers
- Background service worker handles all long-lived tasks and API communication

## Git (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

- NEVER commit directly to main — always feature branch
- Branch naming: feat/xxx, fix/xxx, chore/xxx, docs/xxx
- One branch = one feature or fix
- Conventional commits: feat:, fix:, chore:, docs:, refactor:, test:
- PR required for all changes — squash merge only
- Delete branch after merge

## Testing (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

- Unit tests: Jest on all utils and services (files: *.spec.ts)
- Coverage threshold: 95% (branches, functions, lines, statements — all ≥95%)
- Tests must pass before any PR can be merged
- Mock only external APIs — never mock Chrome extension APIs
- Use jest-environment-jsdom for DOM-dependent tests

## Dependencies (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

- Always use latest stable versions — check npm/pnpm registry before adding any package
- Never pin to old major versions unless there's a documented incompatibility
- Run `pnpm outdated` periodically to stay current
- Minimize dependencies — prefer stdlib and browser APIs over third-party libs
- Audit dependencies weekly: `pnpm audit`

## Tooling (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

- Prettier: run on every file, formatting enforced by pre-commit hook
- Git hooks: commit-msg, pre-push — NEVER skip with --no-verify
- Commit messages: conventional commits enforced by commitlint
- Husky manages all hooks — do not manually modify .git/hooks

## Build Gate (NON-NEGOTIABLE)

- Before presenting ANY code as complete, you MUST run `pnpm build` and it MUST pass with zero TypeScript errors
- If the build fails, fix ALL errors before declaring completion — do not leave them for later
- NEVER commit code that fails `pnpm build` — use `git commit` without `--no-verify`
- The build script (`package.json#scripts.build`) IS THE SINGLE SOURCE OF TRUTH for code quality gates
- Run `pnpm build` after every code generation pass — it's the definitive "am I done?" check

## Operating Rules

- When the user commands "update memory bank", do a full review and update all memory-bank files
- All data processing is local — no multi-tenancy, no user auth needed